This is a greedy list scheduler with a simulation of the timing rules. It has not been compiled or tested.

**Approach**
1. Parse the input and mark every file that some target depends on, directly or transitively. Only these files are worth compiling.
2. Process the needed files in input order, which is already topological because dependencies always appear earlier.
3. Keep, for each file, the earliest time it is available on each server, `avail[f][s]`.
4. Keep, for each server, the time `free[s]` at which it finishes its last step.
5. For a file `f` and server `s`, the start time is `max(free[s], max over dependencies d of avail[d][s])`. The finish time is the start plus the compile time.
6. Choose the server that finishes `f` earliest. Then update `avail[f][s'] = min(existing, finish + r)` for all other servers, and `avail[f][s] = finish` on the chosen server.
7. Memory for `avail` is 1e5 files × 100 servers × 8 bytes = 80 MB, which is fine. Use `long long`.
8. Time is about C × S × deps = 1e5 × 100 × 100 = 1e9 operations in the worst case. That is risky, so I cut it down. Only needed files are scheduled, and the dependency check per server is short-circuited. A faster option is to precompute, for each dependency, `avail[d][s]`, which makes the inner loop simple and cache-friendly if the layout is `avail[f*S + s]`. I iterate servers in the outer loop and dependencies in the inner loop. Over 1e9 simple operations might take a few seconds, so I add a cap: if a file has many dependencies, it is still processed in full, because correctness requires it.

**Target selection:** skipping a target whose estimated finish exceeds its deadline would save work, but it is hard to do correctly, so I compile everything needed. Processing in input order is not deadline-aware, so I order the compilations by the earliest deadline of any target that depends on the file. This is computed backwards through the dependency graph. I then sort the needed files by (deadline key, index). The index tie-break keeps dependency order valid, because a dependency's key is at most that of any dependent's. A dependency with an equal key has a lower index, so it comes first.

The output lists each compile step in that sorted order, which respects the "earlier in the submission" requirement. The server's execution order is the submission order, and the simulation matches this.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    int C,T,S;
    scanf("%d %d %d",&C,&T,&S);
    vector<string> nm(C);
    vector<long long> c(C),r(C);
    vector<vector<int>> dep(C);
    unordered_map<string,int> id;
    id.reserve(C*2);
    char buf[64];
    for(int i=0;i<C;i++){
        long long a,b;
        scanf("%63s %lld %lld",buf,&a,&b);
        nm[i]=buf;c[i]=a;r[i]=b;id[nm[i]]=i;
        int n;scanf("%d",&n);
        for(int j=0;j<n;j++){
            scanf("%63s",buf);
            dep[i].push_back(id[buf]);
        }
    }
    const long long INF=LLONG_MAX/4;
    vector<long long> key(C,INF);
    for(int i=0;i<T;i++){
        long long d,g;
        scanf("%63s %lld %lld",buf,&d,&g);
        int f=id[buf];
        key[f]=min(key[f],d);
    }
    for(int i=C-1;i>=0;i--){
        if(key[i]<INF){
            for(int d:dep[i])key[d]=min(key[d],key[i]);
        }
    }
    vector<int> order;
    for(int i=0;i<C;i++)if(key[i]<INF)order.push_back(i);
    sort(order.begin(),order.end(),[&](int a,int b){
        if(key[a]!=key[b])return key[a]<key[b];
        return a<b;
    });
    vector<long long> avail((size_t)C*S,INF);
    vector<long long> fr(S,0);
    vector<pair<int,int>> out;
    for(int f:order){
        long long best=INF;int bs=0;
        for(int s=0;s<S;s++){
            long long st=fr[s];
            if(st>=best)continue;
            for(int d:dep[f]){
                long long a=avail[(size_t)d*S+s];
                if(a>st){st=a;if(st>=best)break;}
            }
            long long fin=st+c[f];
            if(fin<best){best=fin;bs=s;}
        }
        fr[bs]=best;
        for(int s=0;s<S;s++){
            long long v=(s==bs)?best:best+r[f];
            long long &a=avail[(size_t)f*S+s];
            if(v<a)a=v;
        }
        out.push_back({f,bs});
    }
    printf("%d\n",(int)out.size());
    for(auto&p:out)printf("%s %d\n",nm[p.first].c_str(),p.second);
}
```
